{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de4b8aa20fc16230f14b51", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Oscillations - Test 2", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d61c"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5d6", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d56e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e40fd5f1ef260f84ed1e98"], "concepts": ["6117b8bd67dbe2603c3e8587"], "question": {"text": "A particle executes S.H.M. of amplitude <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>-axis. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, the position of the particle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> and it moves along positive <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>-axis. The displacement of particle in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#948;</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>, then the value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#948;</mi></math> will be"}, "options": [{"id": "52991e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d56f"}, {"id": "9020cf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d570"}, {"id": "89a486", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d571"}, {"id": "10093d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d572"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d573", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e5673b499d1e49588f8b85"], "concepts": ["6117b76a478ae845c43c6d42"], "question": {"text": "An object of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> is executing simple harmonic motion. It amplitude is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and time period (T) is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>. What will be the potential energy of the object at an instant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> starting from mean position. Assume that the initial phase of the oscillation is zero."}, "options": [{"id": "20003a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>62</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d574"}, {"id": "5ba21b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d575"}, {"id": "eae3b2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d576"}, {"id": "725ced", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d577"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d578", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee5"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5df5"], "question": {"text": "The time period of oscillation of a simple pendulum of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math>&nbsp;suspended from the roof of a vehicle, which moves without friction down an inclined plane of inclination <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math>, is given by :"}, "options": [{"id": "652423", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msqrt><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>g</mi><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></mrow></mfenced></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d579"}, {"id": "9f5d4a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msqrt><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>g</mi><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></mrow></mfenced></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d57a"}, {"id": "e33c65", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msqrt><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d57b"}, {"id": "ba4df6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msqrt><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>g</mi><mi>tan</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></mrow></mfenced></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d57c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d57d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4764"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b471a"], "question": {"text": "In simple harmonic motion, the total mechanical energy of given system is $E$. If mass of oscillating particle $P$ is doubled then the new energy of the system for same amplitude is:\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FVDM93q7Lj2g5-7ugw1mKMoOuFpSn3YB7jiXVpIQkPEk.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>"}, "options": [{"id": "d65723", "text": "$E$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d57e"}, {"id": "89890f", "text": "$E / \\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d57f"}, {"id": "d7825a", "text": "$2 E$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d580"}, {"id": "963767", "text": "$E \\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d581"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d582", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e5673b499d1e49588f8b85"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b53"], "question": {"text": "A pendulum is executing simple harmonic motion and its maximum kinetic energy is $\\mathrm{K}_{1}$. If the length of the pendulum is doubled and it performs simple harmonic motion with the same amplitude as in the first case, its maximum kinetic energy is $\\mathrm{K}_{2}$"}, "options": [{"id": "994540", "text": "$K_{2}=2 K_{1}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d583"}, {"id": "897844", "text": "$\\mathrm{K}_{2}=\\frac{\\mathrm{K}_{1}}{2}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d584"}, {"id": "118286", "text": "$\\mathrm{K}_{2}=\\frac{\\mathrm{K}_{1}}{4}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d585"}, {"id": "106edd", "text": "$K_{2}=K$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d586"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d587", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4764"], "concepts": ["6117b607478ae845c43c631c"], "question": {"text": "Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A) : Time period of a simple pendulum is longer at the top of a mountain than that at the base of the mountain.\nReason (R): Time period of a simple pendulum decreases with increasing value of acceleration due to gravity and vice-versa.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "7be9be", "text": "Both \\((\\mathbf{A})\\) and \\((\\mathbf{R})\\) are true and \\((\\mathbf{R})\\) is the correct explanation of \\((\\mathbf{A})\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d588"}, {"id": "dfb6c0", "text": "(A) is true but (R) is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d589"}, {"id": "6b1e7f", "text": "(A) is false but (R) is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d58a"}, {"id": "970ea8", "text": "Both \\((\\mathbf{A})\\) and \\((\\mathbf{R})\\) are true but \\((\\mathbf{R})\\) is not the correct explanation of \\((\\mathbf{A})\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d58b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d58c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e40fd5f1ef260f84ed1e98"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e3e"], "question": {"text": "<p>The point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;moves with a uniform speed along the circumference of a circle of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>36</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and covers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>. The perpendicular projection <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> on the diameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi><mi>N</mi></math> represents the simple harmonic motion of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>. The restoration force per unit mass when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> touches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math> will be :</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fafb054e4-9d68-429e-96e6-bd5245736318-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 148px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "8ecad7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>49</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d58d"}, {"id": "c629ef", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>87</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d58e"}, {"id": "a5e900", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d58f"}, {"id": "cafcdd", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d590"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d591", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee5"], "concepts": ["6117b76a478ae845c43c6d33"], "question": {"text": "A cylindrical plastic bottle of negligible mass is filled with <math><mn>310</mn><mtext>&#8201;</mtext><mi>m</mi><mi>l</mi></math> of water and left floating in a pond with still water. If pressed downward slightly and released, it starts performing simple harmonic motion at angular frequency <math><mi>&#969;</mi><mo>.</mo></math> If the radius of the bottle is <math><mn>2.5</mn><mtext>&#8201;</mtext><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></math> then <math><mi>&#969;</mi></math> is close to: <math><mo>(</mo></math> density of water <math><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></math>"}, "options": [{"id": "aad760", "text": "5.00 rad <math><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sec</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi> </mi></mrow></mrow></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d592"}, {"id": "37ff77", "text": "<math><mn>2.50</mn><mi> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> sec</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow></mrow></mrow></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d593"}, {"id": "53b810", "text": "<math><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">7.9 rad&nbsp;sec</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>&nbsp;</mi></mrow></mrow></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d594"}, {"id": "fecaef", "text": "<math><mn>3.75</mn><mi> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> sec</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow></mrow></mrow></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d595"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d596", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e5673b499d1e49588f8b85"], "concepts": ["6117b90767dbe2603c3e8f87"], "question": {"text": "A block of mass m attached to a massless spring is performing oscillatory motion of amplitude &#39;A&#39; on a frictionless horizontal plane. If half of the mass of the block breaks off when it is passing through its equilibrium point, the amplitude of oscillation for the remaining system become &fnof;A. The value of &fnof; is:"}, "options": [{"id": "6d3f05", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d597"}, {"id": "767747", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d598"}, {"id": "ef4ef9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d599"}, {"id": "81de13", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d59a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d59b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e40fd5f1ef260f84ed1e98"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87d0"], "question": {"text": "A particle is subjected two simple harmonic motions as :<br/>$\\mathrm{x}_1=\\sqrt{7} \\sin 5 \\mathrm{tcm}$ <br/>and $x_2=2 \\sqrt{7} \\sin \\left(5 t+\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\mathrm{cm}$<br/>where x is displacement and $t$ is time in seconds. The maximum acceleration of the particle is $\\mathrm{x} \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~ms}^{-2}$. The value of x is :"}, "options": [{"id": "1da020", "text": "$175$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d59c"}, {"id": "f36215", "text": "$25 \\sqrt{7}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d59d"}, {"id": "7c50fa", "text": "$5 \\sqrt{7}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d59e"}, {"id": "323643", "text": "$125$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d59f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5a0", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4764"], "concepts": ["617aa9cbef3fe65ad8e81c30"], "question": {"text": "The displacement of a damped harmonic oscillator is given by <math><mi>x</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos</mi></mrow><mo>&#8289;</mo><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#966;</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></math> Here <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></math> is in seconds. The time taken for its amplitude of vibration to drop to half of its initial value is close to:"}, "options": [{"id": "695768", "text": "<math><mn>27</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5a1"}, {"id": "f5dc94", "text": "<math><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5a2"}, {"id": "15d747", "text": "<math><mn>13</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5a3"}, {"id": "052982", "text": "<math><mn>7</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5a4"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5a5", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee5"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5df5"], "question": {"text": "Choose the correct length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;versus square of time period <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></math> ) graph for a simple pendulum executing simple harmonic motion."}, "options": [{"id": "ceaf93", "text": "<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fb6aad69a-44c2-4b95-a130-e4c63c0e6dab-9896638.PNG?alt=media\" style=\"width: 251px; height: 200px;\" />", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5a6"}, {"id": "42f5a6", "text": "<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F6443dbaa-636d-4a50-9c95-01ae1fa836a4-9896638_1.PNG?alt=media\" style=\"width: 254px; height: 200px;\" />", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5a7"}, {"id": "b86f94", "text": "<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fb2a6bc8a-ee46-4627-9298-b29624c4c849-9896638_2.PNG?alt=media\" style=\"width: 260px; height: 200px;\" />", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5a8"}, {"id": "a98fe0", "text": "<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F8b3a6642-d65a-41b5-98d7-f6ac5a9870bd-9896638_3.PNG?alt=media\" style=\"width: 257px; height: 200px;\" />", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5a9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5aa", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e40fd5f1ef260f84ed1e98"], "concepts": ["6117b76a478ae845c43c6d2a"], "question": {"text": "When a particle executes SHM, the nature of graphical representation of velocity as a function of displacement is:"}, "options": [{"id": "5f391e", "text": "straight line", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5ab"}, {"id": "ebbd45", "text": "elliptical", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5ac"}, {"id": "0c5255", "text": "circular", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5ad"}, {"id": "785fd3", "text": "parabolic", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5ae"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5af", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4764"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b471a"], "question": {"text": "<p>In figure (A), mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;is fixed on mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;which is attached to two springs of spring constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi></math>. In figure (B), mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;is attached to two spring of spring constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></math>. If mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;in (A) and (B) are displaced by distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>&nbsp;horizontally and then released, then time period <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> corresponding to (A) and (B) respectively follow the relation.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F45749699-b2e8-4db1-b0b1-eda289f4dbe5-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 380px; height: 132px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "89b558", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5b0"}, {"id": "825a2d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5b1"}, {"id": "22017f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5b2"}, {"id": "e2e9ec", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5b3"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5b4", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e5673b499d1e49588f8b85"], "concepts": ["6117b76a478ae845c43c6d42"], "question": {"text": "<p>Which graph represents the difference between total energy and potential energy of a particle executing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>SHM</mi></math>&nbsp;vs its distance from mean position?</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "82a770", "text": "<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fac20f9ff-175b-4c0d-8849-d59fddb77b84.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 140px;\" /></p>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5b5"}, {"id": "7d464b", "text": "<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F28a51046-94f9-4286-b004-e0303e84b61d.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 141px;\" /></p>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5b6"}, {"id": "2298a0", "text": "<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F72b05ac8-0e94-40bc-8b4b-23507f96198e.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 144px;\" /></p>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5b7"}, {"id": "22b32d", "text": "<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fb50dd46a-683d-4335-894c-d27a74f07b72.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 151px;\" /></p>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5b8"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5b9", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee5"], "concepts": ["6117b76a478ae845c43c6d33"], "question": {"text": "<p>In the given figure, a body of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math> is held between two massless springs, on a smooth inclined plane. The free ends of the springs are attached to firm supports. If each spring has spring constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo></math> the frequency of oscillation of given body is:</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F458d011e-1844-444b-a815-bd7c917871fd-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 180px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "415270", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5ba"}, {"id": "449fd1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac><msqrt><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5bb"}, {"id": "543fc7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac><msqrt><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>M</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5bc"}, {"id": "75260c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5bd"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5be", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e40fd5f1ef260f84ed1e98"], "concepts": ["6117b64c478ae845c43c6818"], "question": {"text": "A block of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> attached to a spring is made to oscillate with an initial amplitude of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. After <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> minutes the amplitude decreases to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. Determine the value of the damping constant for this motion. (take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>693</mn></math>&nbsp;)"}, "options": [{"id": "7e9400", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5bf"}, {"id": "4ee7a8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5c0"}, {"id": "d190ab", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5c1"}, {"id": "e53552", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5c2"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5c3", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e5673b499d1e49588f8b85"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b53"], "question": {"text": "A particle oscillates along the $x$-axis according to the law, $x(\\mathrm{t})=x_0 \\sin ^2\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{t}}{2}\\right)$ where $x_0=1 \\mathrm{~m}$. The kinetic energy $(\\mathrm{K})$ of the particle as a function of $x$ is correctly represented by the graph"}, "options": [{"id": "07cbd3", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FUYFlb5kmixH6zTIUQNm3AGbzNiVjIqOhQ3NkIJWJl7g.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/><br/>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5c4"}, {"id": "215984", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FuErf9Yevaw2MZdT8jUUPUu6q4Y2GXpMrir7EYXsXZow.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/><br/>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5c5"}, {"id": "8bf8f4", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F6jot21EZ6X69gqrKbN5cPA0ksi4zJRKBQ32wXe0mE-8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/><br/>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5c6"}, {"id": "d5d1e9", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FN9znVpeMaI7X9QWdfUPmvXbp3IP2pSG_WCLklFPS7rI.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/><br/>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5c7"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5c8", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4764"], "concepts": ["6117b607478ae845c43c631c"], "question": {"text": "Two simple pendulums having lengths $l_1$ and $l_2$ with negligible string mass undergo angular displacements $\\theta_1$ and $\\theta_2$, from their mean positions, respectively. If the angular accelerations of both pendulums are same, then which expression is correct?"}, "options": [{"id": "3b0549", "text": "$\\theta_1 l_2^2=\\theta_2 l_1^2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5c9"}, {"id": "5b1bf9", "text": "$\\theta_1 l_1=\\theta_2 l_2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5ca"}, {"id": "0c7934", "text": "$\\theta_1 l_1^2=\\theta_2 l_2^2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5cb"}, {"id": "5d89b3", "text": "$\\theta_1 l_2=\\theta_2 l_1$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5cc"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5cd", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee5"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5df5"], "question": {"text": "Time period of a simple pendulum in a stationary lift is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math>. If the lift accelerates with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math> vertically upwards then the time period will be<br />(Where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo></math> acceleration due to gravity)"}, "options": [{"id": "1e6b7a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></msqrt><mi>T</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5ce"}, {"id": "1ced7b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac></msqrt><mi>T</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5cf"}, {"id": "4da020", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>7</mn></mfrac></msqrt><mi>T</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5d0"}, {"id": "920f0f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac></msqrt><mi>T</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5d1"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:39.084Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:39.084Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d61a"}, {"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d618", "title": "Physics Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5fc", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e40fd5f1ef260f84ed1e98"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e3e"], "question": {"text": "A body is performing simple harmonic with an amplitude of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The velocity of the body was tripled by air Jet when it is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> from its mean position. The new amplitude of vibration is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is _____ ."}, "options": [{"id": "84ad21", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5fd"}, {"id": "851c4f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5fe"}, {"id": "86fb41", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d5ff"}, {"id": "7ae697", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d600"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d601", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee5"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5df5"], "question": {"text": "<p>A simple pendulum is placed at a place where its distance from the earth's surface is equal to the radius of the earth. If the length of the string is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>, then the time period of small oscillations will be _________<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo></math>take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>]</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "51276d", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d602"}, {"id": "8d7b31", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d603"}, {"id": "5c69c4", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d604"}, {"id": "358ab5", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d605"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d606", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e5673b499d1e49588f8b85"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b53"], "question": {"text": "<p>At a given point of time the value of displacement of a simple harmonic oscillator is given as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></mrow></mfenced><mo>.</mo></math> If amplitude is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and kinetic energy at that time is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;the value of force constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. The value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>&nbsp;is _____.</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "0c910f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d607"}, {"id": "83feb2", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d608"}, {"id": "4dcc3a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d609"}, {"id": "3dfc5f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d60a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d60b", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4764"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b471a"], "question": {"text": "<p>Consider two identical springs each of spring constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi></math> and negligible mass compared to the mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math> as shown. Fig. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> shows one of them and Fig. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> shows their series combination. The ratios of time period of oscillation of the two SHM is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;where value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is ______.</p><p>(Round off to the Nearest Integer)</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F6bebcd64-f773-49be-9c84-55ce15fe9005-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 191px; height: 271px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "8b494d", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d60c"}, {"id": "247343", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d60d"}, {"id": "773fde", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d60e"}, {"id": "52024e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d60f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929d610", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b479a"], "topics": ["60e40fd5f1ef260f84ed1e98"], "concepts": ["6117b64c478ae845c43c6818"], "question": {"text": "A particle of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>250</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math>&nbsp;executes a simple harmonic motion under a periodic force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>25</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>. The particle attains a maximum speed of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> during its oscillation. The amplitude of the motion is ______<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></m